(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-macronutrients-micronutrients-water-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Macronutrients, Micronutrients & Water (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"According to the Food and Nutrition Board, what is the RDA for carbohydrate intake in adults?","choices":["250 grams per day","65 grams per day","130 grams per day","45 grams per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"The RDA for carbohydrate is 130 grams per day for adults — the minimum needed to supply the brain and central nervous system with adequate glucose, though average U.S. intake runs much higher (180–330 g/day). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 35 (Carbohydrate Needs)"},{"q":"Per the Food and Nutrition Board's list of exceptions to standard protein needs, dialysis patients require approximately how much protein per kilogram of body weight, compared to the standard 0.8 g/kg RDA?","choices":["About 0.6 g/kg of body weight","About 2.5 g/kg of body weight","About 1.7 g/kg of body weight","1.1 to 1.4 g/kg body weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Dialysis patients need about 1.1–1.4 g/kg body weight, above the standard 0.8 g/kg RDA, per the slide's list of exceptions to standard protein needs (which also includes endurance athletes and the elderly). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 86 (RDA for Protein)"},{"q":"A fasting blood glucose level is classified as hyperglycemia when it exceeds approximately what value?","choices":["70 mg per 100 mL of blood","180 mg per 100 mL of blood","125 mg per 100 mL of blood","100 mg per 100 mL of blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Per the slide, hyperglycemia is defined as fasting blood glucose greater than 125 milligrams per 100 milliliters of blood. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 32 (Regulating Blood Glucose)"},{"q":"A diabetic patient on insulin develops shakiness, sweating, and confusion a few hours after a meal. Per the slide, the recommended initial treatment is consumption of how many grams of simple carbohydrate?","choices":["5 grams","50 grams","15 grams","30 grams"],"answer":2,"exp":"The slide specifies treating diabetic hypoglycemia with consumption of 15 g of simple carbohydrate, a glucose tablet, or glucagon. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 42 (Hypoglycemia)"},{"q":"A patient develops temporary difficulty digesting lactose during a bout of infectious diarrhea. Per the slide's distinction, this is best classified as which type?","choices":["Lactose intolerance, present in 25% of North American adults","Congenital lactase deficiency","Primary lactose maldigestion, a normal pattern beginning at ages 3–5","Secondary lactose maldigestion, due to a specific temporary cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secondary lactose maldigestion is a temporary condition caused by a specific insult — like diarrhea — to the intestinal lining, distinct from primary maldigestion's normal age-related decline. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 26 (Lactose Maldigestion and Lactose Intolerance)"},{"q":"Consuming more than 60 grams of fiber per day can cause which of the following, per the slide?","choices":["Increased availability of certain minerals","A need for extra fluid intake","Improved energy balance in children","Decreased fecal bulk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Excess fiber intake (more than 60 g/day) requires extra fluid, may decrease the availability of some minerals, and can create unmet energy needs in children, per the slide. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 16 (Too Much Fiber)"},{"q":"Oleic acid, an omega-9 fatty acid, is classified as monounsaturated because its carbon chain contains how many double bonds?","choices":["One","Four or more","Two","Zero"],"answer":0,"exp":"Monounsaturated fatty acids like oleic acid contain exactly one double bond in the carbon chain, per the slide's definition. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 51 (Monounsaturated Fatty Acid Structure)"},{"q":"Alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) and linoleic acid (omega-6) are both classified on the slides as which type of fatty acid?","choices":["Trans","Saturated","Polyunsaturated","Monounsaturated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) and linoleic acid (omega-6) are polyunsaturated fatty acids, each containing more than two double bonds per the slides. — Macro/Micro lecture, slides 52–53 (Polyunsaturated Fatty Acid Structure)"},{"q":"Which food source provides the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids DHA and EPA directly, rather than requiring the body to convert a shorter-chain precursor?","choices":["Fatty fish such as salmon","Walnuts, a plant source","Canola oil, a plant source","Flaxseed oil, a plant source"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fatty fish (salmon, tuna, mackerel, lake trout) and fish oil are direct sources of DHA and EPA per the slide, which recommends about 2 servings of fish per week. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 56 (Omega-3 Fatty Acid Sources)"},{"q":"Hydrogenation of a liquid vegetable oil produces a firmer, more solid fat through what chemical change?","choices":["Addition of hydrogen to a double carbon bond, forming a single bond","Removal of hydrogen atoms from an existing double carbon bond","Addition of an extra fatty acid onto the glycerol backbone","Conversion of cholesterol molecules into a sterol ester form"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hydrogenation adds hydrogen to a double carbon bond, converting it into a single bond, which converts liquid oil into a firmer, more solid fat, per the slide. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 58 (Hydrogenation of Oil)"},{"q":"Per the slide's list of cardiovascular disease risk factors, an HDL level below what value is considered a risk factor?","choices":["60 mg per dL","40 mg per dL","200 mg per dL","100 mg per dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Low HDL — under 40 mg per dL — is listed as a cardiovascular risk factor on the slide, alongside total cholesterol over 200 mg/dL and triglycerides over 200 mg/dL. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 69 (Risk Factors for Cardiovascular Disease)"},{"q":"Why does chronically low protein intake lead to edema, according to the slide on protein and fluid balance?","choices":["Low protein directly increases capillary blood pressure, forcing fluid out of vessels and into surrounding tissue spaces","Protein deficiency increases sodium retention, which then osmotically pulls extra water out of the bloodstream and into tissues","Low protein intake causes the kidneys to stop filtering fluid entirely, so waste and water both build up systemically","Protein molecules are too large to move into tissues, so with too little protein, fluid isn't pulled back into the bloodstream and builds up in tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Protein molecules are normally too large to move out of the bloodstream into tissue, and that size difference is what lets protein pull the proper amount of fluid back into the blood. When protein intake is too low, that pull weakens, fluid builds up in the tissues, and edema results. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 80 (Protein Maintains Fluid Balance)"},{"q":"In developing countries, protein-calorie malnutrition is most associated with which two outcomes, per the slide?","choices":["Elevated LDL cholesterol and hypertension","Stunted growth and increased risk of infection","Obesity and insulin resistance conditions","Excess vitamin A storage and toxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The slide states that in developing countries, protein-calorie malnutrition stunts growth and increases risk of infection — distinct from its comparatively rare occurrence in developed countries, where it's seen mostly in alcoholics or the elderly. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 87 (Protein-Calorie Malnutrition)"},{"q":"According to the slide, which of the following is NOT listed as a nutrient deficiency concern specific to vegan diets?","choices":["Zinc","Vitamin B12","Iron","Vitamin C"],"answer":3,"exp":"The slide lists vitamin B12, vitamin D, calcium, iron, zinc, and omega-3 fatty acids as vegan diet deficiency concerns. Vitamin C is not on that list, since the fruits and vegetables that make up a larger share of a vegan diet are generally rich sources of it. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 93 (Vegan Diets)"},{"q":"Vitamin K is required as a coenzyme in the synthesis of proteins involved in which two processes, per the slide?","choices":["Calcium absorption and phosphorus balance","Blood clotting and bone metabolism","Antioxidant defense and fatty acid breakdown prevention","Vision and gene expression"],"answer":1,"exp":"The slide states vitamin K is required as a coenzyme during the synthesis of proteins involved in blood clotting and bone metabolism. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 100 (Vitamins: Classification & Function—Fat Soluble)"},{"q":"Water-soluble vitamins in food are particularly susceptible to destruction by which of the following, per the slide?","choices":["Heat, light, air exposure, cooking in water, and alkalinity","Freezing and blanching before long-term frozen storage","Ripening on the plant well past peak harvest time","Refrigeration below 40°F for several consecutive weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"The slide states water-soluble vitamins are particularly susceptible to destruction by heat, light, air exposure, cooking in water, and alkalinity, while freezing (with prior blanching) actually helps retain vitamin content. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 105 (Vitamin Preservation in Foods)"},{"q":"Chromium is classified as a trace mineral whose primary listed role, per the slide, is:","choices":["Blood clotting and muscle contraction","Antioxidant defense against oxidative stress","Helping maintain normal blood glucose levels","Component of thyroid hormones"],"answer":2,"exp":"The slide lists chromium's role as helping to maintain normal blood glucose levels, distinguishing it from the other trace minerals discussed nearby (copper, fluoride, manganese, molybdenum). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 112 (Minerals Classification & Function: Trace Minerals)"},{"q":"Per the slide, approximately what percentage of sodium intake in the American diet comes from salt added during food manufacturing, rather than the salt shaker at home?","choices":["About 50%","About 95%","About 25%","About 77%"],"answer":3,"exp":"About 77% of sodium intake is added as salt during food manufacturing, per the slide — which is why packaged, processed, restaurant, canned, and frozen prepared foods are the biggest sodium sources, while fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and unprocessed meats are comparatively low in sodium. — Macro/Micro lecture, slides 146–147 (Getting Enough Sodium / Food Sources of Sodium)"},{"q":"Which of the following can cause a potassium deficiency, per the slide?","choices":["Laxative abuse","Excess dietary potassium intake","High blood pressure","Vitamin D toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The slide lists laxative abuse, along with chronic diarrhea, vomiting, alcohol abuse, eating disorders, and very low-calorie diets, as causes of potassium deficiency, with symptoms including muscle cramps, confusion, constipation, and irregular heartbeat. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 151 (Potassium: Deficiency)"},{"q":"Which statement about hard versus soft water is accurate, per the slide?","choices":["Soft water is unsafe to drink, while hard water is always considered safe","Hard water contains high levels of calcium and magnesium; soft water contains a high level of sodium","Hard and soft water differ only in their fluoride content levels","Hard water contains high levels of sodium; soft water contains high levels of calcium and magnesium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The slide defines hard water as containing high levels of calcium and magnesium (present in about 89% of U.S. homes, and it makes soap lather poorly), while soft water contains a high level of sodium and produces cleaner-rinsing laundry. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 140 (What Is the Difference Between Hard and Soft Water?)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what causes lactose maldigestion, and how common is the primary form in North American adults?","choices":["A reduction in the lactase enzyme; primary lactose maldigestion is present in about 25% of North American adults","An excess of the lactase enzyme, affecting nearly all adults","A bacterial infection of the small intestine in most children","A deficiency of stomach acid, affecting under 1% of adults"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: reduced lactase causes lactose maldigestion, and the primary form affects about 25% of North American adults. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 26"},{"q":"How does the lecture recommend treating diabetic hypoglycemia?","choices":["Restrict all carbohydrate until symptoms pass","Consume about 15 grams of simple carbohydrate, such as a glucose tablet, or give glucagon","Give a high-fat, high-protein meal and wait several hours","Administer insulin immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: diabetic hypoglycemia is treated with about 15 grams of simple carbohydrate (or glucagon). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 42"},{"q":"The lecture classifies palm, palm kernel, and coconut oils (\"tropical oils\") as which type of fat?","choices":["Omega-3 polyunsaturated fat","Trans fat produced by hydrogenation","Saturated fat","Monounsaturated fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: tropical oils such as palm and coconut are considered saturated fats. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 59"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which are the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids and their recommended source intake?","choices":["Alpha-linolenic acid from red meat, eaten daily","Linoleic acid from tropical oils, eaten twice daily","Oleic acid from olive oil, at five servings per week","Docosahexaenoic acid and eicosapentaenoic acid from fatty fish, at about two servings of fish per week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: docosahexaenoic acid and eicosapentaenoic acid come from fatty fish, with about two servings per week recommended. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 56"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which plant food provides all nine essential amino acids?","choices":["Soy products such as soy milk, tofu, and soybeans","White rice alone","Iceberg lettuce","Apples"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: soy is a plant source that provides all nine essential amino acids. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 74"},{"q":"Per the lecture, how does inadequate protein intake lead to edema?","choices":["Low protein raises blood glucose, drawing water into tissues","Proteins normally attract fluid back into the bloodstream; when protein is too low, fluid builds up in the tissues","Low protein increases red blood cell production, flooding tissues","Excess protein blocks the kidneys, causing swelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: blood proteins pull fluid back into vessels, so low protein lets fluid accumulate in tissues as edema. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 80"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which vitamins pose the greatest toxicity risk, and which single vitamin's toxicity is most frequently observed?","choices":["Minerals only, because vitamins are never toxic","All vitamins equally, with no single one standing out","Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E), because they are not readily excreted; vitamin A toxicity is observed most often","Water-soluble vitamins, because they are stored in fat; vitamin C toxicity is most common"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: fat-soluble vitamins are not readily excreted, and vitamin A toxicity is the most frequently observed. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 104"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which trace mineral is a component of the thyroid hormones?","choices":["Calcium","Fluoride","Sodium","Iodine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: iodine is a component of the thyroid hormones. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 111"},{"q":"According to the lecture, about how much sodium is in one teaspoon of salt, which also equals the tolerable upper limit?","choices":["About 2,300 milligrams","About 500 milligrams","About 100 milligrams","About 4,700 milligrams"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: one teaspoon of salt contains about 2,300 milligrams of sodium, equal to the tolerable upper limit. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 146"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) eating pattern?","choices":["Focused on eliminating all dairy products","High in calcium, potassium, and magnesium and low in fat and sodium, with abundant fruits and vegetables","High in sodium and saturated fat to raise energy","Very low in fruits and vegetables"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the DASH pattern is high in calcium, potassium, and magnesium and low in fat and sodium, rich in fruits and vegetables. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 158"}]};
